2026-06-30 15:19:16,908 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-b1b66e1e-c196-4d94-97e2-704dd25e1fd8', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n20180921_QExHFX4_RSLC3_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_Lig_D.raw\n20180921_QExHFX4_RSLC3_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_noLig_D.raw\n20180921_QExHFX4_RSLC3_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_noLig_DMSO_D.raw\n20180921_QExHFX4_RSLC3_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_noLig_M.raw\n20190215_Lumos_RSLC6_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_A30P_D.raw\n20190215_Lumos_RSLC6_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_A30P_M.raw\n20190215_Lumos_RSLC6_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_A30P_T.raw\n20190215_Lumos_RSLC6_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_A53T_D.raw\n20190215_Lumos_RSLC6_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_A53T_M.raw\n20190215_Lumos_RSLC6_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_A53T_T.raw\n20190215_Lumos_RSLC6_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_G51D_D.raw\n20190215_Lumos_RSLC6_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_G51D_M.raw\n20190215_Lumos_RSLC6_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_G51D_T.raw\n20190215_Lumos_RSLC6_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_WT_D.raw\n20190215_Lumos_RSLC6_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_WT_M.raw\n20190215_Lumos_RSLC6_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_WT_T.raw\n20190708_QExHFX3_RSLC2_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_s10_aSyn_Lipo_dimer_25p.raw\n20190708_QExHFX3_RSLC2_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_s11_aSyn_Lipo_monomer_15p.raw\n20190708_QExHFX3_RSLC2_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_s12_aSyn_Lipo_DMSO_trimer_30p.raw\n20190708_QExHFX3_RSLC2_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_s13_aSyn_Lipo_DMSO_dimer_25p.raw\n20190708_QExHFX3_RSLC2_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_s14_aSyn_Lipo_DMSO_monomer_15p.raw\n20190708_QExHFX3_RSLC2_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_s15_aSyn_Lipo_ligand_trimer_30p.raw\n20190708_QExHFX3_RSLC2_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_s16_aSyn_Lipo_ligand_dimer_25p.raw\n20190708_QExHFX3_RSLC2_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_s17_aSyn_Lipo_ligand_monomer_15p.raw\n20190708_QExHFX3_RSLC2_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_s5_aSyn_Bic_monomer_7p.raw\n20190708_QExHFX3_RSLC2_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_s6_aSyn_SDS_monomer_5p.raw\n20190708_QExHFX3_RSLC2_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_s7_aSyn_Lipo_monomer_10p.raw\n20190708_QExHFX3_RSLC2_Schwarz_Konrat_MFPL_coom_s9_aSyn_Lipo_trimer_30p.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nChemical Crosslinking of Isotopically Labeled α-Synuclein Reveals Structural Changes and the Interface of Oligomeric Species\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nDue to its high pharmaceutical relevance, α-synuclein is one of the best studied intrinsically disordered proteins to date. Its structural plasticity reaches from α-helical when bound to membranes through disordered in solution to β-sheet when forming amyloid fibrils. Recently the possible toxicity of membrane bound α-synuclein oligomers has elicited increased interest in the understanding of its membrane bound state. Both the high structural variability in the bound state and the requirement of membranes to elicit structural conversion of α-synuclein into is oligomeric membrane bound state have impeded the determination of a high-resolution oligomeric membrane-bound structure. The use of chemical crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) in this context leads to a very large set of peptide spectrum matches (PSMs), which do not permit the elucidation of a single, well defined structure based on XL-MS alone. The pattern of PSMs obtained does, however, represent possible links within the ensemble of membrane bound α-synuclein states.  Here, we use fully 15N isotopically labeled α-synuclein in 1:1 mixtures with the natural abundance (14N) protein in order to allow distinction between intermolecular and intramolecular crosslinks in α-synuclein oligomers. Information obtained on the main crosslinking regions as well as changes in the observed PSM patterns is then used in conjunction with data obtained from nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) measurements to develop high-resolution structural models of α-synuclein. We expect that the approach taken here will also prove useful in other highly flexible oligomeric systems.\n\n[METHODS]\nCrosslinking reactions of α-synuclein were carried out in similar conditions as published previously (doi:10.1021/acs.jpcb.1c01281). As required 50 µM of 14N α-synuclein or 50 µM of a 1:1 istotopic 15N:14N mixture of α-synuclein bound to 1 mg/ml of POPG based liposomes was incubated with 2 mM 1-ethyl-3-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)carbodiimide (EDC) and 5 mM Hydroxy-2,5-dioxopyrrolidine-3-sulfonic acid (Sulfo-NHS) for 60 min at room temperature for all crosslinking experiments containing liposomes (produced as published, doi:10.1093/brain/aww238). Conditions containing bicelles or SDS-micelles were generated with 100 µM of isotopically mixed α-synuclein and 15 mM bicelles or and 40 mM SDS, respectively. Bicelles for aggregation and subsequent crosslinking reactions were composed of 10 mM DHPC and 5 mM DMPG. All reactions were subjected to SDS-PAGE after being stopped with 50 mM Tris and 20 mM β-mercaptoethanol. The high SDS content in the loading buffer and incubation at 70 °C for 1min dissolved POPG liposomes prior to loading the samples on the PAGE-gel. Select bands of the monomer, dimer and trimer forms were excised. subjected to SDS-PAGE and bands corresponding to mono- and dimer bands were excised. The Coomassie-stained gel band was destained with a 1:1 mixture of acetonitrile (Chromasolv®, Sigma-Aldrich) and 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate (Sigma-Aldrich). After reduction with 10 mM dithiothreitol (Roche) and alkylation with 50 mM iodoacetamide, trypsin (Promega; Trypsin Gold, Mass Spectrometry Grade) and chymotrypsin (Promega sequencing grade) were used for proteolytic cleavage. Digestion was carried out with trypsin at 37 °C overnight and subsequently with chymotrypsin at 25 °C for 5 h. Formic acid was used to stop the digestion. Peptides were extracted in the ultrasonication bath and desalted using C18 Stagetips (doi:10.1038/nprot.2007.261).  Peptides were analysed on an UltiMate 3000 HPLC RSLC nano system (Thermo Fisher Scientific) coupled to a Q Exactive HF-X or an Orbitrap Fusion Lumos mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific), equipped with a Nanospray Flex ion source (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Equal amounts of the samples were loaded on a trap column (Thermo Fisher Scientific, PepMap C18, 5 mm × 300 μm ID, 5 μm particles, 100 Å pore size) at a flow rate of 25 μL/min using 0.1% TFA as mobile phase. After 10 min, the trap column was switched in-line with the analytical C18 column (Thermo Fisher Scientific, PepMap C18, 500 mm × 75 μm ID, 2 μm, 100 Å) and peptides were eluted applying a segmented linear gradient from 2% to 80% solvent B (80% acetonitrile, 0.1% formic acid; solvent A 0.1% formic acid) at a flow rate of 230 nL/min over 120 min. The mass spectrometer was operated in data-dependent mode, survey scans were obtained in a mass range of 350-1650 m/z with lock mass activated, at a resolution of 120,000 at 200 m/z and an AGC target value of 3E6 (HF-X) or 4E5 (Lumos). The 10 most intense ions (HF-X) were selected with an isolation width of 1.6 Thomson for a max. of 250 ms, fragmented in the HCD cell at 28% (HF-X) or 30% (Lumos) collision energy and the spectra recorded at a target value of 1E5 and a resolution of 60,000. Peptides with a charge of +1,+2 or >+7 were excluded from fragmentation, the peptide match feature was set to preferred, the exclude isotope feature was enabled, and selected precursors were dynamically excluded from repeated sampling for 20 seconds within a mass tolerance of 8 ppm.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
